2 mins video

hook: ai augmented traffic lights
part 1: hardware
-> electronics
- defined requirements (off just power) and limiting factors (28 leds + 8 buttons, 50mA max output)
- constructed electrical circuits (bjt current amplifier / gpio pull up resistor) 
- purchased components following datasheets, optimising for construction at home
- placed and routed PCB footprints, ordered them and soldered them together

-> mechanical
- ideate a practical and robust layout 
- designed 3d models and printed structural pieces, taking into account rigidity, ease of printing, and modularity
- selected a base material (MDF), marking out and drilling holes
- screwed and tightened assembly for the whole structure

-> electrical
- selected cable dimensions, routed, and organised them alongside mechanical supports
- twisted and tightened screw connectors with appropriate strain relief, for long-term reliability. 
- did some fault finding and resolved issues with root cause analysis 

part 2: software
- interfaced with hardware (GPIO + usb)
- created abstractions over my set of traffic light classes (set_red to mean red on, yellow off, green off)
- implemented a time and event driven state machine
- enabled stream-based safe-limited serial communications (protocol design with delimiter, length, crc + parsing)
- connected with an AI api key (google ai studio)
- constructed prompts to respond in a parsable format (space separated format)
- handled the streamed response in a multi-threaded graphical user application

conclusion:
which means my traffic lights can react with the flexibility of AI, while still being bound by human-defined safety limits
